-- title:
--   Linear disjointness of a function field from constant field extensions
-- statement:
--   Let $K$, $F$, $K'$, $F'$ be fields equipped with $K$-algebra structures on $F$, on $K'$ and on $F'$, a $K'$-algebra structure on $F'$ and an $F$-algebra structure on $F'$, compatible in the sense that $K \to K' \to F'$ and $K \to F \to F'$ are both scalar towers (so $F'$ contains commuting copies of $K'$ and of $F$ over $K$). Assume $K$ is algebraically closed. Assume further that $F$ is a function field of one variable over $K$, in the sense that there is an element $x \in F$ transcendental over $K$ with $F$ finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$; that $F'$ is likewise a function field of one variable over $K'$, i.e. some $x \in F'$ is transcendental over $K'$ with $F'$ finite-dimensional over $K'(x)$; and that $F'$ is generated over $K'$ by the image of $F$, i.e. the intermediate field of $F'/K'$ generated by the range of $\operatorname{algebraMap} F F'$ is all of $F'$. Then for every index type $\iota$ and every family $b : \iota \to F$ that is linearly independent over $K$, the image family $i \mapsto \operatorname{algebraMap} F F' (b\,i)$ is linearly independent over $K'$.
--
--   This is the linear disjointness of $F$ and $K'$ over $K$ for a constant field extension $F' = F\cdot K'$ of an algebraic function field of one variable with algebraically closed constant field $K$, valid in any characteristic; equivalently, the natural map $F \otimes_K K' \to F'$ is injective. It underlies the comparison of a curve over $K$ with its base change to $K'$ — places, divisors and regular differentials — and is invoked by the statements on compatibility of places, of the $\ell$-function bookkeeping and of Cartier-fixed regular differentials under constant field extension. The proof cites [`AlgebraicCurve.exists_separating_transcendental_of_perfectField`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.exists_separating_transcendental_of_perfectField), which produces a separating transcendence element for $F$ over the perfect field $K$.
